WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.819
Saturation and contamination, with the documented cases.

2
00:00:04.099 --> 00:00:14.381
MMLU (Hendrycks and colleagues, 2020) asked multiple-choice questions across 57 subjects and was the standard knowledge benchmark for years.

3
00:00:14.661 --> 00:00:28.848
It saturated: frontier models now score near the ceiling, and Hugging Face showed in 2023 that different but reasonable ways of running the same MMLU evaluation could change scores and even rankings.

4
00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:42.547
Harder successors such as Humanity’s Last Exam (Phan and colleagues, 2025) were built from questions that frontier models failed at creation time, which buys a few years before the same thing happens.

5
00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:50.421
Contamination is the more insidious problem, because a contaminated benchmark can look unsaturated and still mislead.

6
00:00:50.701 --> 00:01:03.501
Deng and colleagues (2023) masked a wrong answer option in benchmark questions and found that commercial models could often reproduce the missing option, a strong sign the items had been seen.

7
00:01:03.781 --> 00:01:23.493
Zhang and colleagues (2024) wrote GSM1k, a fresh set of grade-school math problems in the style of the public GSM8K, and found that some model families scored noticeably worse on the fresh set, consistent with overfitting to the public one, while the strongest models showed little drop.

8
00:01:23.773 --> 00:01:28.083
SWE-bench Verified is the clearest 2026 case.

9
00:01:28.363 --> 00:01:42.549
SWE-bench (Jimenez and colleagues, 2023) asks agents to resolve real GitHub issues and checks fixes with the repository’s tests; Verified was a human-filtered 500-task subset.

10
00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:49.720
On 23 February 2026, OpenAI announced it would no longer report Verified scores.

11
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.848
Its audit of 138 problems that its o3 model did not consistently solve found 59.

12
00:01:57.128 --> 00:02:06.173
4% had material flaws in the tests or descriptions, and it concluded that recent gains increasingly reflected exposure during training.

13
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:24.907
It recommended SWE-bench Pro (Deng and colleagues at Scale AI, 2025): 1,865 tasks from 41 repositories, with a public set, a held-out set, and a commercial set from private codebases, designed to resist contamination.

14
00:02:25.187 --> 00:02:34.808
Pro has its own critics on grader reliability, which is the normal life cycle of a benchmark: introduced, trusted, saturated, audited, replaced.
